  "name": "Question 2",
  "problem": "Given that $a$ is an **irrational** number, which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $\\frac{1}{7}$ is irrational\n\nII. $\\frac{1}{7}+a$ is always irrational\n\nIII. $a-\\frac{1}{7}$ is sometimes rational and sometimes irrational",
  "worked_solution": "Recall that: \n\n- the sum of two rational numbers is *always* rational:\n\n$$\n\\text{rational}+\\text{rational}=\\text{rational}\n$$\n\n- the sum of a rational number and an irrational number is *always* irrational:\n\n$$\n\\text{rational}+\\text{irrational}=\\text{irrational}\n$$\n\n- the sum of two irrational numbers is *sometimes* rational and *sometimes* irrational.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the statements.\n\n- Statement I is false.\n\n- Statement II is true. Since $\\frac{1}{7}$ is rational and $a$ is irrational, their sum is always an irrational number.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $\\frac{1}{7}$ is rational and $a$ is irrational, their difference is always an irrational number.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"",
